## Law in force on 1 October 2025

The applicable document is the **Residential Tenancies Act 1997, Authorised Version 110, dated 6 August 2025**. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

- The property had to be advertised or offered at a **fixed rent**. The provider or agent must not solicit or invite an offer higher than the advertised rent. A breach carries a penalty of **60 penalty units for a natural person or 300 penalty units for a body corporate**. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- Advertising the property at one rent and then listing it at a higher rent is **not, by itself, expressly addressed** in the retrieved provisions. The Act does prohibit a provider or agent covered by section 30G from making a **false or misleading representation about the rent**, with the same penalties. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- The Act does not specifically regulate an email wrongly saying that an applicant withdrew, and it does not require the provider to accept your application, explain its selection decision, or offer you the property. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- The misleading-conduct provision in section 30E concerns conduct that induces a person to **enter into a residential rental agreement**. On the facts given, you did not enter one, so the retrieved document does not establish that this provision applies to the withdrawal email. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

## What you can challenge

You may have a basis to raise the matter as a possible contravention of the fixed-rent and no-higher-offer rules **if the agent or provider solicited, invited, or accepted a higher offer in connection with the advertised property**. However, merely hearing that another person offered more does not establish that the agent or provider solicited or invited that offer. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

The documents do not provide a specific application process, complaint pathway, right to compensation, or prescribed remedy for the circumstances you describe. They also do not state what evidence an applicant must preserve. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

## What to preserve

Although the Act does not prescribe an evidence list, preserve records that could establish:

- the original advertisement, including the advertised rent;
- the date and time you applied;
- your application and any confirmation of receipt;
- the email saying you had withdrawn;
- any messages showing that you did not withdraw;
- the later advertisement and its higher rent;
- any written statement or other record about a higher offer; and
- the names of the provider, agent and advertising platform.

Keep the original electronic files where possible, together with screenshots showing the URL, date and time. These records would be relevant to whether the rent was advertised as fixed, whether a higher offer was solicited or invited, and whether the advertising was misleading. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
